le y\\le 2-x}$\n- **II.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\,0<y<1+x}$\n- **III.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8ea4da6-07e1-57b7-a83b-7aaa01037d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472472} {:answer/id #uuid "4b72b35d-e95a-594a-9f80-c223cf007810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ea4da6-07e1-57b7-a83b-7aaa01037d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472472} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b0b7b5-216a-546d-8ada-6354495eb0a1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473631} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62bb0d7-c664-52e5-962a-8f1a4a27c301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472474} {:answer/id #uuid "987df7de-06e3-5899-9ac7-26468bcfaf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987df7de-06e3-5899-9ac7-26468bcfaf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472475} :answer-field/id #uuid "26d4a480-241b-594b-a24f-7feaa874aaa8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f73c605-275a-55b6-8e5a-e9bd0344391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472476} {:answer/id #uuid "95048fe1-1692-52bf-b42e-39fccbdf29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95048fe1-1692-52bf-b42e-39fccbdf29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472477} :answer-field/id #uuid "046b1c2d-c127-5bfe-9da5-5e118283b7ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1346b272-ab34-50a3-8e2c-0cbfe3ad7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105231" :question/problem "![](images/37/373448202bfd022f5629219b2c176f4096e4eeae275c1095798d77be92122cc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(1)=4$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $2\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)=2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $4\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c7a1d5-2624-5583-aeae-da6b722a9800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473548} {:answer/id #uuid "d811fc76-0af3-5686-b258-9ec94ee426b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c7a1d5-2624-5583-aeae-da6b722a9800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473548} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bfbf6e-2545-5f52-9799-41c7bca12360" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81660a94-f669-50bb-b01a-01293ad51c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473550} {:answer/id #uuid "51f4519c-df1c-5ed7-887b-6f1eca40896e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81660a94-f669-50bb-b01a-01293ad51c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473550} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d06d02-6bed-5ad7-8f32-70dcdb1c3125" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f56e9ea-8cf4-54d7-8527-7f8faf227ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473552} {:answer/id #uuid "4a669770-c352-5a86-88f4-78cbb1e23d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a669770-c352-5a86-88f4-78cbb1e23d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473553} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9581ab-c4fe-5bba-9e72-95a487a5dfad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2021b7c7-5d84-5929-874c-8d6923b11db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-90878" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n\nII. $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f(\\sqrt[3]{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{3}=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={e}^{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g({e}^{y})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y})=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddfaf89-9322-5a84-a50e-62aa07b28c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473560} {:answer/id #uuid "551a09d1-398a-58c1-94f9-f0fdcf86b67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "551a09d1-398a-58c1-94f9-f0fdcf86b67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10319f6-e3ce-51db-9398-cfeb826e35f6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e06e772-1e77-53fb-909f-e91be4a700e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d750378-c7c6-5ba3-beb9-58671cf4ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e06e772-1e77-53fb-909f-e91be4a700e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473562} :answer-field/id #uuid "639df046-b26b-596d-ab4d-ff5cf881eb13" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483d3aae-3043-5e83-859a-284e4fd1571c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473564} {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfe423e-2133-5abd-80c5-484043bc3e63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fb6cc00-0fe3-5564-afaa-6bddf30c9f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90955" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe1a6204-e5ec-5f44-b26e-7ccc55915e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473542} {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9131fc67-75e4-563c-b8d0-94a23a385951" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} {:answer/id #uuid "fad30099-8449-5c4a-8a50-0b3446240d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37b982e-6668-5b7d-b9a0-36b334e068a8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} {:answer/id #uuid "0098fe9d-5f1e-546e-a0ec-56802952db84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d089faf-62fc-5a4e-ad21-0bf3a5219729" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" :question/math-academy-id "q-90789" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(2)=4$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} {:answer/id #uuid "da2eb5f7-1baa-53f4-ad79-0d1d55247851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} :answer-field/id #uuid "74710deb-3884-595d-a92d-aa098b3250f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} {:answer/id #uuid "07e54daa-2cf8-5d76-b9fc-ac27c1ee1152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55e82c7-9120-59da-8986-81de94c17491" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8197f8-ea17-52e0-ada2-12a7ef638766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473558} {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f071064-8b41-5c17-83e0-821190cb7676" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d76a2452-4488-5b99-ace5-042b8edc218d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90902" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$\n\nII. $h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$\n\nIII. $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} {:answer/id #uuid "fb500e02-911c-5c8b-9f78-e88f6e8c72fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cec2b1e-72b6-50a1-855d-2902cfa078a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7073623d-5ce7-509c-a188-db7013873ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472480} {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481} :answer-field/id #uuid "d770e4eb-8dda-5703-95a9-2c2d291bcb35" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bb4560-454b-5e25-81d8-5d5901fcb0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472482} {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b741a97-1773-51a6-a938-029c480ebbf0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-105246" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(1)=7$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fffb5d-3a48-5bb6-ac88-ce930684ca97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473434} {:answer/id #uuid "294eeec8-975d-5c82-ae0f-2771583b8dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "