:answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e91f8fe9-c6f2-558a-bf39-b0389019d5ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-86021" :question/problem "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[7]_{11}?$\n\nI. $18$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $15$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[7]_{11} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=7+11k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,7-2\\cdot 11,\\,7-11,\\,7,\\,7+11,\\,7+2\\cdot 11,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-15,-4,7,18,29,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $18$ belong to $[7]_{11},$ while $15$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787f9f37-580f-5658-9df9-ce826b611451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} {:answer/id #uuid "c4403496-69e1-5d08-b02d-1b03668dd8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787f9f37-580f-5658-9df9-ce826b611451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1a3fb6-945f-5879-9865-fdc5a922c3f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7460c1a4-dd63-595d-8732-ee34d9a2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} {:answer/id #uuid "71f5c96a-5c5b-5605-9ce0-b480c9528139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7460c1a4-dd63-595d-8732-ee34d9a2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} :answer-field/id #uuid "a767f879-2f51-5809-aebb-daff65b44383" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a97ed2f-d3d1-546a-874d-42ae2f24fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472500} {:answer/id #uuid "be2ff147-060f-5c7b-81e0-38256805dec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2ff147-060f-5c7b-81e0-38256805dec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3046741-713a-58b0-a1a8-53bf395bbfbb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e5966d3-ce4d-5835-ab91-1f478e587602" :question/math-academy-id "q-109510" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b50a042-fd0e-5d6e-bb0f-935ead60737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473518} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7d15ef-aaf6-55d7-982b-505c97a0f6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b50a042-fd0e-5d6e-bb0f-935ead60737a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473518} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c92a1b6-0af5-5cc3-a70f-34e4b7402c1a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf2fbc9-664b-5c12-9f73-578676d097bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473520} {:answer/id #uuid "41efc32c-150f-59ff-afb4-a285f47f9892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41efc32c-150f-59ff-afb4-a285f47f9892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473521} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7230e64-9d33-5d1e-868c-637788a6c48f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e959c24-0b7b-5159-be20-0712f3305ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473522} {:answer/id #uuid "29d89754-823e-51b8-a1b3-06fb6113390e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29d89754-823e-51b8-a1b3-06fb6113390e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cc23dd-00ba-5f1f-95ae-d41251462553" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" :question/math-academy-id "q-88693" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5901841-e8c8-5cbf-a277-7f5d6bd154ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472508} {:answer/id #uuid "89815d70-f7a8-5a3d-8dc6-edd3a9b414b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5901841-e8c8-5cbf-a277-7f5d6bd154ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472508} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad307b8-c32d-51ae-9cc5-239fd502f244" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "675b79aa-0b55-56b0-b75a-127b830e9a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472510} {:answer/id #uuid "a52bb3cf-32df-58c4-893f-1fc229df6b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "675b79aa-0b55-56b0-b75a-127b830e9a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472510} :answer-field/id #uuid "2847c887-d4df-5a65-acf5-493a754fad3e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef07f37d-ed46-50c3-8961-01e5ac788b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472512} {:answer/id #uuid "33cab82b-32ec-5fa0-9569-1db28a6ff758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33cab82b-32ec-5fa0-9569-1db28a6ff758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2e79f4d-084b-5b53-8f38-886deae491b2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115272" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac55073-69fa-5d04-beb1-b62f89911c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473052} {:answer/id #uuid "474df447-a674-59d3-8567-60b8dfff4e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac55073-69fa-5d04-beb1-b62f89911c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473052} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e96549-c627-5583-a9fb-cf1da90d12c3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd70a287-a637-5426-81c9-78d3016dd9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473054} {:answer/id #uuid "caf9bc9c-885d-592e-b0ad-f4214345b3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd70a287-a637-5426-81c9-78d3016dd9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473054} :answer-field/id #uuid "d01f4732-ff58-597f-8c0f-c64f05d02e95" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b720fa-eff2-5721-8737-d3949780bca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473056} {:answer/id #uuid "07107876-6eb0-508b-a5c8-b915a1c3f6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07107876-6eb0-508b-a5c8-b915a1c3f6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473057} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3487039-0ced-5468-aee8-6fe1bf8ee226" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331097" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **II.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- **III.** ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "529383e0-630b-588e-8db9-4ce7c7068aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473058} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4e76cc-dcfa-518c-812e-99530bece3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f4e76cc-dcfa-518c-812e-99530bece3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473059} :answer-field/id #uuid "58cd9a6e-53ff-55c8-9379-9163ca8db7db" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67701f92-2f97-5a77-bf09-984c8a18dfbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473060} {:answer/id #uuid "37f862dc-0eca-58e7-9512-63967c674760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67701f92-2f97-5a77-bf09-984c8a18dfbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473060} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa1aae3-5835-594e-bfcc-c8f9c5407254" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0188e982-24c3-5077-b63d-055de0590758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473062} {:answer/id #uuid "44480db1-81c7-5fae-9f45-49d3d2b427e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44480db1-81c7-5fae-9f45-49d3d2b427e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473063} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb6c655-3b68-5cc9-ace6-9170c6aa1506" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331980" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a19bac-653a-5eee-9c2f-41a4b7bbc01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472874} {:answer/id #uuid "2f193e35-b8be-5ea4-bf86-0a381a9f2e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f193e35-b8be-5ea4-bf86-0a381a9f2e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472875} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5dfd31-b4df-5ee3-a703-03f13f39eb84" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473832} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b808580-7a64-5663-b6ca-9281d1ff47e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472876} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa6bc08-886b-529d-9d06-e2bde7d8ac56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b808580-7a64-5663-b6ca-9281d1ff47e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472876} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbacaa8-ff71-5012-8acb-759fa0f15267" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa3885af-7f7c-5d34-a944-d8f36b8f0d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472878} {:answer/id #uuid "be577392-cb10-5612-af5e-b71a08d83712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa3885af-7f7c-5d34-a944-d8f36b8f0d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472878} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c19d87-12f9-58f4-a383-06319f467bdd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93d6ac86-9246-53ae-8020-58de7dcc7070" :question/math-academy-id "q-213094" :question/problem "|  | $✠$ | $♣$ | $♠$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $✠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♣$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined on the set $\\{✠,♣,♠\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(✠,♣)$\n\nII. $(♣,♠)$\n\nIII. $(♠,✠)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b0e90e-a9a2-53c3-a98d-d5ff68d7b96c" :answer/type {