ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56431f13-81e3-5ed9-a1fb-9d4c1abe031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472699} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbb5113-3ef0-579a-97fa-79761be4e54a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b59e493b-eefe-51a6-8a6a-b1643795634d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139806" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n- **I.** ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}$\n- **II.** $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218618